---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="About Us — DigitalDramaa"
  description="How DigitalDramaa's Archive and Commentary sections work, and who's behind the original analysis."
  keywords={["DigitalDramaa", "about digitaldramaa", "internet drama site", "gaming drama news", "tech scandal news"]}
  monetize
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <span class="eyebrow">About</span>
    <h1>About DigitalDramaa</h1>
    <p>Two sections, kept separate: an hourly Archive, and original Commentary.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>The Archive</h2>
    <p>
      The Archive is an automated headline tracker. It continuously scans a
      set of gaming, tech, and internet-culture feeds and, when a genuinely
      new story shows up, adds a short entry — headline, source, and a link
      straight to the original reporting. No rewritten article body, no
      AI-generated opinion. It exists to catch things early and to help us
      find what's worth writing about, not to publish commentary itself,
      which is why Archive pages aren't indexed by search engines.
    </p>

    <h2>Commentary</h2>
    <p>
      Commentary is where DigitalDramaa actually publishes. Every post is
      written under the <strong>DigitalDramaa Editorial</strong> byline, and
      every post naming a real person or organization is reviewed by a human
      editor before it goes live — see our{" "}
      <a href="/editorial-policy">editorial policy</a> for the full rules on
      how that works, sourcing, and corrections.
    </p>

    <h2>How AI Is Used Here</h2>
    <p>
      AI helps with research and drafting — finding stories, gathering
      sources, and putting together a first pass. It does not publish
      anything on its own, and it does not write opinions about named real
      people without a human editor reviewing them first. That review is
      what separates Commentary from the Archive.
    </p>

    <h2>Questions or Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      See our <a href="/editorial-policy">editorial policy</a> for how we
      handle claims about real people and corrections, our{" "}
      <a href="/terms-and-conditions">Terms & Conditions</a> and{" "}
      <a href="/privacy-policy">Privacy Policy</a> for more detail, or{" "}
      <a href="/contact">contact us</a> directly with questions or correction
      requests.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page-head {
    padding: var(--sp-32) 0 var(--sp-24);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    margin-bottom: var(--sp-32);
  }

  .eyebrow {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent);
    background: var(--accent-wash);
    padding: 3px 9px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }

  .page-head h1 {
    font-size: 2.4rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.2rem;
  }

  .page-head p {
    font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .prose {
    max-width: 72ch;
    padding-bottom: var(--sp-64);
  }

  .prose h2 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    margin: var(--sp-32) 0 0.6rem;
  }

  .prose h2:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .prose p {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    max-width: none;
  }

  .prose a {
    color: var(--accent);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
</style>
